  "id": "eval.2026-09-08-comp-134600.llm-inference-gateway",
  "title": "2026-09-08-comp-134600 · llm-inference-gateway",
  "description": "# LLM inference gateway\nClients use one endpoint and request a capability tier rather than a backend. Read across the upper row from identity and admission to prompt preparation, cache lookup and tier routing; backend outcomes descend into delivery, which reads right to left.\n\n- Admission enforces tenant concurrency and token rate. Excess work queues only until its deadline; expiration explicitly rejects it and asks the caller to retry.\n- Prompt assembly applies tenant context and safety policy. The cache serves exact or deterministic semantic matches, keyed by prompt, parameters and policy version.\n- Routing uses residency, live health, latency, queue depth and price within the requested tier. Capacity includes independently accounted hosted providers and a separately scheduled self-hosted pool. Circuit breakers drain traffic to healthy capacity and use trickle probes to recover.\n- Before the first token, failures are classified and retryable attempts return to tier fallback with a fresh deadline and the same idempotency key. The request ID follows every attempt. Non-retryable or exhausted attempts return errors. Once streaming begins, complete or visibly fail without silently retrying, preserving received tokens.\n- Accounting records tokens, latency, backend, cache outcome and cost in a billing stream and redacts prompts/responses for audit. Spend control first selects the cheapest capable option, then rejects new work.\n- A small structured evaluation sample is mirrored to an isolated shadow backend; its output never changes the client response.\n\nSolid wires show request/response paths; dashed wires show accounting, control and shadow traffic.",
